<h2>Fire in the Pine Barrens</h2>
<p>The Pine Barrens stretch across parts of Burlington, Ocean, Atlantic, Camden, Cape May, Cumberland and Gloucester counties. Dry springs with low humidity and wind are prime fire season. In 2007 the Warren Grove fire burned roughly 17,000 acres and forced evacuations in Ocean County. In April 2025 the Jones Road Wildfire burned about 15,000 acres near Lacey and Ocean townships. Most wildfires never reach a house. When they do, damage reaches far beyond the burn line.</p>
<h2>Damage that’s easy to miss</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ember damage</strong> to roofs, gutters, decks and vents on homes that never caught fire.</li>
<li><strong>Radiant heat</strong> that warps vinyl siding, cracks windows and melts fixtures facing the fire.</li>
<li><strong>Smoke and ash</strong> drawn through HVAC systems, attics and soft furnishings.</li>
<li><strong>Landscaping and trees</strong>, usually covered only to a limited percentage under the policy.</li>
<li><strong>Additional living expenses</strong> during evacuations and cleanup.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Building the evidence</h2>
<p>Wildfire smoke claims are often denied as “no visible damage.” We document heat distortion, soot deposits and odor, and we rely on qualified testing when it helps establish the scope. The goal is a valuation the panel can follow, room by room and elevation by elevation.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Living near the forest</b>Pinelands communities include Browns Mills, Medford Lakes, Barnegat, Manchester, Hammonton and dozens of smaller towns. If you were evacuated and came home to smoke odor, document it before cleaning begins.</div>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My house didn’t burn but smells like smoke. Is that a claim?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="ans"><p>Smoke damage is commonly covered under homeowners policies. The dispute is usually about how much cleaning, sealing or replacement is needed. Document odor, soot and any HVAC contamination early.</p></div></details><details><summary>Are trees and landscaping covered after a wildfire?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="ans"><p>Many homeowners forms cover trees, shrubs and plants for fire, but only up to a small percentage of the dwelling limit and a per-plant cap. Check your policy’s additional coverages.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you work in the Pinelands towns specifically?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="ans"><p>Yes. We inspect throughout Burlington, Ocean and Atlantic counties, including communities bordering state forests and wildlife management areas.</p></div></details></div><h2>Related services</h2><div class="related"><a class="rel" href="/appraisal-practice/tree-fall-ice-storm-claims/"><small>Service</small>Tree Fall &amp; Ice Storm Appraisal<svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 